Script 1 — qlrt.sh
- No SSH AllowUsers restriction that could unexpectedly block logins.
- SSH configuration is tested before being reloaded, with backups for rollback.
- UFW rules are added before enabling the firewall, and existing firewall rules aren't reset.
- PAM is configured using Debian's PAM profile manager instead of blindly editing the authentication stack.
- APT refuses moving release aliases such as stable and trixie.
- Package upgrades use --no-remove and never use dist-upgrade.
- Only Apache's service is disabled when present; its package is retained.
- All suspicious accounts and files are reported for review instead of blindly deleted.
Debian's own documentation confirms that apt-get upgrade doesn't remove installed packages, and --no-remove makes APT abort if a requested operation would remove one


Script 2 — 02_CHANGE_PASSWORDS.sh
This retains password behavior, but adds two safeguards: it checks all 14 accounts before changing any password, and skips both the detected automatic-login account and the account you're currently using.

It does not use the insecure security1 password for hhogan.
